# Batch 38 (2026-11-25): medieval Islamic world and Persia; Japan and Korea; Arctic, Caribbean and Pacific; Andes, 18th-19th c. science and 21st-century sky scares
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None,statusNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  if statusNote:d['statusNote']=statusNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: the medieval Islamic world and Persia
E+=[
X('thousand-and-one-nights','The Thousand and One Nights: Scheherazade\'s stories and the tales added in Paris',M,'myth-legend',2,850,['textual','literary history'],
 "The Thousand and One Nights (Alf Layla wa-Layla, the 'Arabian Nights') is a collection of Middle Eastern folktales compiled in Arabic over many centuries. Its frame story, in which Scheherazade saves her life by telling King Shahryar a new tale every night, is drawn from a lost Middle Persian book, Hezār Afsān ('A Thousand Tales'), which itself had Indian elements; in Iraq in the 9th-10th centuries Arab stories were added, including tales set at the court of the Abbasid caliph Harun al-Rashid, and later layers came from Mamluk Egypt and Syria. A 9th-century paper fragment of the opening pages is among the oldest surviving pieces of Arabic paper. There is no single canonical text: manuscripts and printed editions differ widely in which stories they contain.",
 [('Orphan tales','Two of the most famous \'Arabian Nights\' stories, "Aladdin" and "Ali Baba and the Forty Thieves", are not found in any earlier Arabic manuscript. The French translator Antoine Galland added them to his edition after the Syrian Maronite storyteller Hanna Diyab told them to him in Paris in 1709. Whether Diyab drew on oral tradition he heard in Aleppo or partly created them is debated.'),
  ('A window on real history?','Some historians use the Nights as evidence for everyday life, trade and attitudes in medieval Baghdad and Cairo; others caution that the tales mix fantasy (jinn, rocs, magic lamps) with idealised memory, and that Harun al-Rashid\'s court in them is largely legendary.'),
  ('Orientalist lens','Literary scholars note that European translations (Galland 1704-17, Lane, Burton 1885) shaped Western images of the "exotic East" as much as they reflected the Arabic originals.')],
 ['wiki:One_Thousand_and_One_Nights','wiki:Hanna_Diyab','wiki:Antoine_Galland'],img='One Thousand and One Nights',end=1709,dateNote='Compiled c. 800s-1400s; Galland\'s French edition 1704-1717'),
X('ibn-sina-canon','Ibn Sina (Avicenna) and the Canon of Medicine',H,'confirmed',2,1025,['textual'],
 "Ibn Sina (c. 980-1037), known in Europe as Avicenna, was a Persian polymath born near Bukhara who served at the courts of several Iranian rulers. He wrote around 450 works, about 240 of which survive, on philosophy, medicine, astronomy, logic and more. His Canon of Medicine, a five-book medical encyclopedia completed in 1025, organised Greek (especially Galen), Persian and Indian medical knowledge with his own observations; it was translated into Latin in 12th-century Toledo and served as a standard textbook in European universities into the early modern period. His philosophical encyclopedia, the Book of Healing, made him one of the most influential Aristotelian thinkers of the Muslim world.",
 [('Father of experimental medicine?','Some modern writers credit the Canon with early rules for testing drugs (for example, testing on more than one case and observing timing of effects) and with recognising contagion and quarantine; historians of medicine caution against reading modern clinical-trial ideas back into a work that still rested on Galenic humoral theory.'),
  ('Rejected and revered','In 1527 the Swiss physician Paracelsus reportedly burned a copy of the Canon to show his break with ancient authority; at the same time it remained a core text of Unani medicine, still practised in South Asia today.'),
  ('Whose heritage?','Iran, Uzbekistan, Tajikistan and the Arab world all celebrate Ibn Sina as a national or civilisational figure; he wrote mainly in Arabic but also in Persian.')],
 ['wiki:Avicenna','wiki:The_Canon_of_Medicine','url:https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ibn-sina/|Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy: Ibn Sina [Avicenna]|encyclopedia'],img='File:Avicenna canon 1597.jpg',end=1037,dateNote='Canon completed 1025; Ibn Sina c. 980-1037'),
X('al-biruni-earth-radius','Al-Biruni measures the Earth from a hilltop at Nandana',H,'confirmed',3,1020,['textual','tested by experiment'],
 "Abu Rayhan al-Biruni (973-c. 1050), a polymath from Khwarazm (in today's Uzbekistan) who worked at the court of Mahmud of Ghazni, devised a new way to measure the size of the Earth. At the fort of Nandana (in today's Pakistan) he measured the height of a hill and then, from its top, the angle by which the horizon dipped below the horizontal; with trigonometry, these two numbers give the Earth's radius. His result was close to the modern value. He also wrote a remarkable study of Indian religion, science and society (Tarikh al-Hind, c. 1030), comparing Hindu and Greek ideas, and discussed whether the Earth might rotate on its axis.",
 [('How accurate really?','Popular accounts often say his radius was within about 1-2% of the modern figure. Historians of science note that atmospheric refraction alters the dip angle and that the result depends on how his units are converted, so the true precision of the method was probably lower and the close match partly fortunate.'),
  ('First anthropologist?','Al-Biruni is often called the first anthropologist or founder of comparative religion for his attempt to describe Indian beliefs "exactly as they are"; others note that he also wrote from the position of a scholar in a conquering court.'),
  ('Replications','Modern amateurs and teachers have repeated his hill-and-horizon method as a classroom experiment, with results of varying accuracy depending on refraction.')],
 ['wiki:Al-Biruni','wiki:India_(Al-Biruni)'],img='Al-Biruni',dateNote='Measurement c. 1020s'),
X('al-jazari-automata','Al-Jazari\'s Book of Ingenious Mechanical Devices (1206)',H,'confirmed',3,1206,['textual','tested by experiment'],
 "Ismail al-Jazari (1136-1206) was chief engineer at the Artuqid court in upper Mesopotamia (today south-east Turkey), as his father had been. In 1206 he finished the Book of Knowledge of Ingenious Mechanical Devices, describing 50 machines with illustrations and instructions for building them: water-raising pumps, water clocks such as the famous 'elephant clock' (an Indian elephant carrying a tower with a mahout, birds, serpents and a turbaned figure that move on the hour), hand-washing automata and a boat of musician automata to entertain guests. His work built on earlier traditions, including the Greek writers Hero and Philo and the 9th-century Banu Musa brothers of Baghdad.",
 [('Father of robotics?','Al-Jazari is sometimes called the "father of robotics"; historians note that his figures were driven by water, floats and cams rather than anything resembling modern control systems, and that the label is a modern honour rather than a historical lineage.'),
  ('Programmable music?','Some researchers have argued that his musician automata used pegs on a rotating drum that could be rearranged to change the rhythm, making it an early "programmable" machine; this interpretation of the text is debated.'),
  ('Rebuilt today','Working replicas of the elephant clock were built in the 2000s, including one at the Ibn Battuta Mall in Dubai, showing that his designs work as described.')],
 ['wiki:Ismail_al-Jazari','wiki:Banu_Musa'],img='Ismail al-Jazari'),
X('sack-of-baghdad-1258','The Mongol sack of Baghdad (1258) and the river black with ink',H,'confirmed',1,1258,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In January-February 1258 a Mongol army under Hulegu, grandson of Genghis Khan, besieged Baghdad, capital of the Abbasid Caliphate. After the walls were breached the city surrendered and was sacked for about a week; the caliph al-Musta'sim was killed - according to the sources wrapped in a carpet and trampled by horses, to avoid shedding royal blood. Estimates of the dead range from tens of thousands to the hundreds of thousands claimed by medieval writers. The fall of the city ended the Abbasid caliphate in Baghdad and has traditionally been seen as the end of the Islamic Golden Age.",
 [('The river of ink','A famous later account says so many books from Baghdad\'s libraries were thrown into the Tigris that the river ran black with ink. Historians such as Michal Biran have shown that libraries in Baghdad reopened for teaching within two years, and that the astronomer Nasir al-Din al-Tusi, who travelled with Hulegu, saved many manuscripts for his new observatory at Maragheh. The image is now often treated as a symbolic exaggeration.'),
  ('End of a golden age?','The traditional view sees 1258 as the end of Islamic scientific flourishing. Many historians argue instead that learning continued strongly in Mamluk Egypt, Ilkhanid Iran, Timurid Samarkand and the Ottoman world, so "decline after 1258" is a simplification.'),
  ('Death toll','Medieval figures of 800,000 to 2 million dead are generally considered inflated; modern estimates are far lower but still very large.')],
 ['wiki:Siege_of_Baghdad_(1258)','wiki:Hulagu_Khan'],img='Siege of Baghdad (1258)',dateNote='29 January - 10 February 1258'),
X('isra-and-miraj','The Isra and Mi\'raj: Muhammad\'s Night Journey to Jerusalem and the heavens',M,'myth-legend',2,621,['textual','religious tradition'],
 "The Isra and Mi'raj are the Islamic narratives of the Prophet Muhammad's Night Journey: carried in one night from Mecca to 'the farthest mosque' (identified with Jerusalem) on the winged steed Buraq, then ascending through the seven heavens, meeting earlier prophets, seeing paradise and hell, and receiving the command for the daily prayers before returning. The Quran contains only brief allusions (the opening verse of sura 17 and verses of sura 53); the detailed story developed in hadith and biographies, first assembled into a connected narrative about two centuries later. The event is commemorated each year on Laylat al-Mi'raj and underlies the sanctity of Jerusalem's Al-Aqsa mosque and the Dome of the Rock in Islam.",
 [('Body or vision?','Muslim scholars have long debated whether the journey was physical, spiritual or a dream-vision; the majority Sunni view holds it was bodily, while others, citing early reports, saw it as a vision.'),
  ('Dante\'s source?','In 1919 the Spanish scholar Miguel Asín Palacios argued that the Mi\'raj literature influenced Dante\'s Divine Comedy; the discovery of the Book of the Ladder, a Mi\'raj text translated into Latin and French at the Castilian court in the 13th century, gave the idea support, though how directly Dante used it is still debated.'),
  ('Cross-cultural pattern','Comparative religion scholars place it among heavenly ascent narratives such as Enoch\'s journey, the Persian Arda Viraf, and shamanic sky journeys; believers regard it as a unique miracle.')],
 ["wiki:Isra'_and_Mi'raj","wiki:Book_of_Muhammad's_Ladder"],img="Isra' and Mi'raj",dateNote='Traditionally c. 620-621'),
X('year-of-the-elephant','The Year of the Elephant: Abraha\'s march on Mecca (c. 570)',H,'disputed',2,570,['textual','epigraphic','religious tradition'],
 "Islamic tradition dates the birth of the Prophet Muhammad to the 'Year of the Elephant', when Abraha, the Aksumite-backed Christian ruler of Himyar in Yemen, marched on Mecca with an army including at least one war elephant to destroy the Kaaba. According to the Quran's Sura al-Fil and later accounts, the lead elephant, Mahmud, knelt and refused to advance, and flocks of birds (ababil) pelted the army with stones of baked clay, destroying it. Abraha himself is a real, well-documented king: his inscriptions in South Arabia record dam repairs at Ma'rib and military campaigns deep into central Arabia, including one dated 552.",
 [('Historical core, debated date','Many historians accept that a South Arabian expedition under Abraha reached the Hijaz, but Abraha\'s own inscriptions point to campaigns around 552, two decades before the traditional date of 570; some therefore think the story attached a famous earlier campaign to the year of Muhammad\'s birth, or that the dating of his birth is symbolic. A recently found Himyarite inscription that may mention elephants has been suggested as support for the campaign.'),
  ('Disease as the birds?','Ibn Ishaq reports that smallpox and measles first appeared in Arabia that year, and some modern writers suggest an epidemic struck the army; this is a naturalistic reading that believers do not need and historians cannot confirm.'),
  ('Religious view','For Muslims the destruction of the army is a miracle protecting the Kaaba, foreshadowing the Prophet\'s mission.')],
 ['wiki:Year_of_the_Elephant','wiki:Abraha'],img='Year of the Elephant',dateNote='Traditionally c. 570; Abraha\'s inscriptions date campaigns to the 540s-550s',statusNote='Abraha confirmed; date and miracle disputed'),
X('al-hakim-disappearance','Al-Hakim, the "Mad Caliph" who vanished in the hills (1021)',H,'confirmed',2,1021,['textual'],
 "Al-Hakim bi-Amr Allah (985-1021?) was the sixth Fatimid caliph, ruling Egypt and much of North Africa and the Levant from Cairo. His reign became famous for sudden, contradictory decrees - bans on certain foods, strict rules on women in public, curbs on night-time business and entertainment - and for persecution of Christians and Jews, including the 1009 order to demolish the Church of the Holy Sepulchre in Jerusalem, an act later cited among the grievances behind the Crusades. He also founded the Dar al-Ilm, a public library and academy in Cairo. On the night of 13 February 1021 he rode out alone, as he often did, into the Muqattam hills east of Cairo and never returned; his donkey and later his bloodstained clothes were reportedly found. His fate remains unknown.",
 [('Murder theory','Medieval chroniclers suspected a plot by his powerful sister Sitt al-Mulk, who became regent for his young son; modern historians note that there is no direct evidence implicating her.'),
  ('Occultation and return','The Druze faith, which emerged in 1017-18 and held that the divine was manifested in al-Hakim, teaches that he did not die but went into concealment and will return at the end of time - one of several "hidden leader" beliefs in Islamic and other traditions.'),
  ('Mad or misunderstood?','He has been portrayed as the "Nero of Egypt" by hostile sources and as a just, ascetic ruler by followers; some historians argue that many of his measures had political or religious logic that was later caricatured.')],
 ['wiki:Al-Hakim_bi-Amr_Allah','wiki:Druze'],img='Al-Hakim bi-Amr Allah',dateNote='Reigned 996-1021; disappeared 13 February 1021'),
X('zahhak-serpent-king','Zahhak: the Persian king with serpents growing from his shoulders',M,'myth-legend',2,-1000,['textual','mythological','comparative mythology'],
 "Zahhak (Avestan Aži Dahāka) begins in the Zoroastrian Avesta as a three-headed dragon-demon, created by the evil spirit Angra Mainyu. In Ferdowsi's Shahnameh (completed 1010) he becomes a tyrant king: tempted by the devil Iblis, who kisses his shoulders, he grows two black serpents from them that must be fed human brains every day. He overthrows the legendary king Jamshid and reigns for a thousand years until the blacksmith Kaveh raises his leather apron on a spear as a banner of revolt (the origin of the legendary Derafsh Kaviani standard of Iran), and the hero Fereydun strikes him with an ox-headed mace and chains him in a cave beneath Mount Damavand, where he must remain until the end of the world.",
 [('Echo of a real king?','Medieval and modern writers have tried to identify Zahhak with a historical tyrant: Ferdowsi makes him an Arab prince; others linked him to the Median king Astyages (Armenian tradition calls Astyages "Azhdahak") or to Babylonian rulers. Most scholars see him primarily as a mythological dragon later "historicised".'),
  ('Indo-Iranian dragon','Comparative mythologists link Aži Dahāka with the Vedic serpent Ahi slain by Indra and with a wider Indo-European dragon-slaying myth; the chained monster awaiting the end of the world parallels Loki and Fenrir in Norse myth and Amirani in Georgian legend.'),
  ('Medical reading','The 14th-century historian Abu al-Fida suggested the "serpents" were moving tumours on his shoulders; this is one of many rationalising explanations of the story.')],
 ['wiki:Zahhak','wiki:Kaveh_the_Blacksmith','wiki:Shahnameh'],img='Zahhak',dateNote='Avestan texts c. 1000-500 BCE (date uncertain); Shahnameh 1010 CE'),
X('tusi-couple-maragheh','The Maragheh observatory and the Tusi couple: an Islamic route to Copernicus?',H,'confirmed',2,1259,['textual','mathematical','instrumental'],
 "In 1259 the Mongol ruler Hulegu, a year after sacking Baghdad, founded an observatory at Maragheh in north-west Iran under the Persian scholar Nasir al-Din al-Tusi. It gathered astronomers from across Eurasia, including China, and built a large library and instruments; its observations produced the Ilkhanic astronomical tables (Zij-i Ilkhani). Already in 1247 al-Tusi had devised the 'Tusi couple', a small circle rolling inside a circle twice its size, which turns two circular motions into a straight-line motion. Astronomers of the so-called Maragha school used it and similar devices to remove the 'equant' from Ptolemy's planetary models.",
 [('Copernicus\'s hidden debt?','Nearly three centuries later Copernicus used the same geometric device, and his lunar and Mercury models closely resemble those of the Damascus astronomer Ibn al-Shatir (14th century). Many historians (for example George Saliba and F. Jamil Ragep) argue that Copernicus must have learned of these models, possibly via Byzantine Greek translations such as those of Gregory Chioniades or through Italian scholars; no direct chain of transmission has been found.'),
  ('Independent discovery','Other historians think Copernicus, a skilled geometer facing the same problem, could have reinvented the device independently; the case is circumstantial.'),
  ('Not heliocentric','The Maragha astronomers kept the Earth at the centre; their innovations were geometric, so the debate concerns Copernicus\'s tools rather than his Sun-centred idea.')],
 ['wiki:Tusi_couple','wiki:Maragheh_observatory','wiki:Nasir_al-Din_al-Tusi'],img='File:Maraga_observatory.jpg',dateNote='Tusi couple 1247; observatory founded 1259'),
]
# --- part 2: Japan and Korea
E+=[
X('forty-seven-ronin','The forty-seven rōnin: the Akō vendetta of 1703 and the legend of Chūshingura',H,'confirmed',2,1701,['textual'],
 "In 1701 Asano Naganori, lord of Akō, drew his sword and wounded the senior court official Kira Yoshinaka inside Edo Castle, reportedly after being insulted; drawing a weapon in the castle was a capital offence and Asano was ordered to commit seppuku that same day, his domain confiscated and his samurai made rōnin (masterless). Led by Ōishi Kuranosuke, a group of them waited almost two years, then on the night of 30-31 January 1703 (14th day of the 12th month of Genroku 15) broke into Kira's Edo mansion, killed him and carried his head to Asano's grave at Sengaku-ji temple. Amid wide public sympathy, the shogunate ordered 46 of them to commit seppuku instead of being executed as criminals; the youngest messenger, Terasaka Kichiemon, survived. They are buried together at Sengaku-ji in Tokyo.",
 [('History versus Chūshingura','Because the shogunate banned staging current events, playwrights disguised the story; the 1748 puppet play Kanadehon Chūshingura, set in the 14th century with changed names, became the most famous version and fixed many details (Ōishi\'s feigned debauchery in Kyoto, romances) that are dramatic inventions rather than documented history.'),
  ('Model of loyalty or of lawlessness?','Already in the 18th century Confucian scholars argued over whether the rōnin were heroes of loyalty or criminals who should have killed Kira immediately or not at all; in the 20th century the story was promoted as a model of bushidō, including by wartime nationalism, which shapes how it is read today.'),
  ('The insult','What Kira actually did to provoke Asano is not recorded; theories range from demands for bribes to personal slights to a dispute over salt production, and some historians suggest Asano was ill or unstable.')],
 ['wiki:Forty-seven_rōnin','wiki:Chūshingura','wiki:Sengaku-ji'],img='Forty-seven rōnin',end=1703,dateNote='Attack in Edo Castle April 1701; vendetta 30-31 January 1703 (Gregorian)'),
X('michizane-tenjin','Sugawara no Michizane: the exiled scholar whose angry ghost became the god of learning',M,'myth-legend',2,903,['textual','religious tradition'],
 "Sugawara no Michizane (845-903) was a brilliant scholar, poet and minister of the Heian court. In 901 his rival Fujiwara no Tokihira had him demoted and exiled to Dazaifu in Kyushu, where he died in 903. In the following decades Tokihira died young, plague and drought struck the capital, sons of Emperor Daigo died, and in 930 lightning struck the Seiryōden hall of the imperial palace, killing several officials; Emperor Daigo fell ill and died months later. The court attributed these disasters to Michizane's vengeful spirit (onryō), posthumously restored his rank and in 947 founded the Kitano Tenmangū shrine in Kyoto to appease him. As Tenjin he became one of Shinto's most popular gods - the patron of scholarship, visited by millions of students before exams.",
 [('Historical reading','Historians see the onryō belief as a way Heian society explained a run of real deaths, epidemics and storms, and as political commentary: deifying the wronged minister implicitly criticised the Fujiwara regents who ruined him.'),
  ('Believers\' view','In Shinto tradition the disasters were genuine signs of Michizane\'s angry spirit, and enshrinement transformed a curse into a blessing; one of the "Three Great Onryō of Japan" with Taira no Masakado and Emperor Sutoku.'),
  ('The flying plum','A legend says his beloved plum tree in Kyoto flew after him to Dazaifu (the tobi-ume, still shown at Dazaifu Tenmangū); a more prosaic version says a seedling was transplanted there.')],
 ['wiki:Sugawara_no_Michizane','wiki:Tenjin_(kami)','wiki:Kitano_Tenmangū'],img='Sugawara no Michizane',dateNote='Died 903; Seiryōden lightning 930; Kitano shrine 947'),
X('abe-no-seimei','Abe no Seimei: the court diviner said to be the son of a fox',M,'myth-legend',3,960,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "Abe no Seimei (921-1005) was a real official of the Heian court, an onmyōji: a specialist in Onmyōdō, a system of divination, calendar-making, astrology and protective ritual derived from Chinese yin-yang and five-elements thought. He served several emperors, read omens, chose auspicious days and performed rites against misfortune, and his descendants, the Tsuchimikado family, controlled the court's Bureau of Onmyō for centuries. After his death he became a legendary wizard: stories credit him with commanding invisible spirit servants (shikigami), defeating rival sorcerers such as Ashiya Dōman and exposing demons; later legends make his mother Kuzunoha a white fox (kitsune) who returned to the forest when her nature was discovered.",
 [('Real official, legendary wizard','Historians distinguish the documented career of a long-lived court technician from the medieval and Edo-period tales, plays and modern manga and films (such as the 2001 film Onmyoji) that turned him into a "Japanese Merlin".'),
  ('The five-pointed star','A pentagram called the Seiman or Seimei kikyō became his emblem and is displayed at the Seimei Shrine in Kyoto, founded in 1007 on the site of his house; similar star symbols appear as protective signs in many cultures.'),
  ('Fox ancestry','Fox-wife stories, in which an animal wife bears a human child and leaves when discovered, occur across East Asia and resemble selkie and swan-maiden tales elsewhere; scholars see Seimei\'s fox mother as a later legend explaining his powers.')],
 ['wiki:Abe_no_Seimei','wiki:Onmyōdō'],img='Abe no Seimei',end=1005,dateNote='921-1005'),
X('okiku-plate-mansion','Okiku and the Plate Mansion: the ghost who counts to nine',M,'myth-legend',3,1741,['oral tradition','textual','theatre'],
 "Banchō Sarayashiki ('The Dish Mansion at Banchō') is one of Japan's most famous ghost stories (kaidan). In its many versions a servant, Okiku, is blamed for losing or breaking one of ten precious plates, is killed - often thrown into a well - and returns as a vengeful spirit (onryō) who rises at night to count the plates from one to nine and then shrieks for the missing tenth. Folk versions set the story at Himeji Castle, where a well is still shown as 'Okiku's Well'; other versions set it in the Banchō district of Edo. It reached the stage as a bunraku puppet play in 1741 and many kabuki adaptations followed, including Okamoto Kidō's 1916 version.",
 [('Origin of the long-haired well ghost','The image of a pale woman with long black hair rising from a well became a classic of Japanese horror; the ghost Sadako in Koji Suzuki\'s Ring novels and films is widely regarded as drawing on Okiku.'),
  ('The Okiku insect','In 1795 wells were infested by a pupa that looks as if tied with threads; people called it the "Okiku bug" and believed it was Okiku reborn - an example of a natural phenomenon absorbed into a legend.'),
  ('Real murder behind it?','Local traditions in Himeji and Edo name supposed historical people and dates, but no contemporary record of a real Okiku has been confirmed; folklorists treat it as a widespread tale type.')],
 ['wiki:Banchō_Sarayashiki','wiki:Onryō'],img='Banchō Sarayashiki',dateNote='First staged 1741; folk versions older'),
X('shimabara-rebellion','The Shimabara Rebellion (1637-38): Amakusa Shirō and Japan\'s Christian uprising',H,'confirmed',2,1637,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In December 1637 peasants and masterless samurai of the Shimabara peninsula and Amakusa islands in Kyushu, many of them Catholic Christians, rose against crushing taxes and the violent persecution of Christianity by the local lord Matsukura Katsuie. They were led by a charismatic teenager, Amakusa Shirō, whom followers saw as a heaven-sent saviour, and fortified the ruined Hara Castle. A shogunate army of over 125,000 besieged them; a Dutch ship even bombarded the castle in support. When Hara fell in April 1638, about 37,000 rebels and sympathisers were killed or beheaded. Matsukura was executed - the only daimyo beheaded in the Edo period - and the shogunate expelled the Portuguese and tightened its 'closed country' policy, driving Christianity fully underground.",
 [('Religious war or tax revolt?','Historians debate whether the rising was mainly a Christian holy war, as the shogunate portrayed it to justify a ban, or an economic revolt by starving farmers in which Christianity provided leadership and hope; most see a mixture of both.'),
  ('Shirō the prophesied boy','Stories claimed a Jesuit missionary had foretold a 16-year-old boy who would save the Christians, and miracles were attributed to Shirō; how much of this the rebels believed and how much was later legend is uncertain.'),
  ('Archaeology at Hara','Excavations at Hara Castle have found crosses, rosary beads and medals alongside mass graves of the defenders, confirming the Christian identity of many and the scale of the massacre.')],
 ['wiki:Shimabara_Rebellion','wiki:Amakusa_Shirō','wiki:Hara_Castle'],img='Shimabara Rebellion',end=1638,dateNote='17 December 1637 - 15 April 1638'),
X('ryukyu-kingdom','The Ryukyu Kingdom: the "bridge of nations" swallowed by Japan (1429-1879)',H,'confirmed',2,1429,['textual','archaeological'],
 "The Ryukyu Kingdom united Okinawa island in 1429 under Shō Hashi and grew rich as a maritime trading hub linking China, Japan, Korea and Southeast Asia; a bronze bell cast for Shuri Castle in 1458 proclaimed the kingdom a 'bridge between nations'. It was a tributary of Ming and Qing China, but in 1609 the Japanese Satsuma domain invaded and made it a vassal while keeping it nominally independent - a 'dual subordination' that let Japan trade indirectly with China. Commodore Perry visited in 1853. Meiji Japan turned the kingdom into a domain in 1872 and annexed it outright as Okinawa Prefecture in 1879, deposing the last king, Shō Tai.",
 [('Annexation contested','China (Qing) protested the 1879 annexation, and some Chinese commentators still question Japan\'s sovereignty claims rooted in it; Japan regards the matter as long settled. Some Okinawan activists describe the "Ryukyu disposition" as colonisation and advocate greater autonomy.'),
  ('Distinct languages and religion','Ryukyuan languages are now recognised by UNESCO as endangered separate languages rather than Japanese dialects, and the indigenous religion centred on noro priestesses and sacred groves (utaki) survives; many scholars see Ryukyu as a distinct civilisation rather than a Japanese periphery.'),
  ('A kingdom of shows','Satsuma made Ryukyuan envoys wear Chinese-style dress and hide any knowledge of Japanese when parading through Edo, to emphasise Ryukyu\'s foreignness - an example of how the kingdom\'s identity was staged by its rulers.')],
 ['wiki:Ryukyu_Kingdom','wiki:Ryukyu_Disposition','wiki:Shuri_Castle'],img='File:Former Shuri Castle Seiden Bell.jpg',end=1879,dateNote='1429-1879'),
X('heo-hwang-ok','Heo Hwang-ok: the princess from "Ayuta" who sailed to Korea',M,'myth-legend',2,48,['textual','genetic','oral tradition'],
 "According to the Samguk yusa, a 13th-century Korean chronicle compiled by the monk Iryeon from an earlier lost Record of Garak, in 48 CE a 16-year-old princess named Heo Hwang-ok arrived by ship with a red sail from the distant kingdom of 'Ayuta' and married King Suro, founder of Geumgwan Gaya in south-east Korea. She is said to have borne him twelve children and to have lived to the age of 157. Millions of Koreans of the Gimhae Kim, Gimhae Heo and Incheon Yi clans trace descent from the couple, and a tomb in Gimhae is venerated as hers. A popular modern view identifies Ayuta with Ayodhya in India; South Korea and India have built a memorial to her there.",
 [('Indian princess','Supporters cite the name\'s similarity to Ayodhya, a stone pagoda said to have been brought on her ship, twin-fish motifs at Suro\'s tomb and a 2004 study that found a mitochondrial lineage in ancient Gimhae remains more common in South Asia; the princess has become a symbol of Korea-India friendship.'),
  ('Sceptical view','Many Korean historians note that the story exists only in the late, Buddhist-coloured Samguk yusa (the older Samguk sagi does not mention her), that Ayodhya is far inland, that the twin-fish motif has other origins, and that the genetic lineage is too widespread to pinpoint India; they see a later legend elevating the Gaya royal line, perhaps linked to Buddhism\'s Indian prestige.'),
  ('Other homelands','Alternative suggestions place Ayuta in Thailand (Ayutthaya, though that city was founded only in 1350), in China\'s Sichuan, or treat it as purely symbolic. The romantic name "Suriratna" often used today comes from a modern Indian comic, not ancient sources.')],
 ['wiki:Heo_Hwang-ok','wiki:Suro_of_Geumgwan_Gaya','wiki:Samguk_yusa'],img='File:Queen Suro Tomb.jpg',dateNote='Traditionally arrived 48 CE; recorded 13th century'),
X('emille-bell','The Emille Bell: Korea\'s greatest bell and the legend of the child in the molten bronze',M,'myth-legend',3,771,['archaeological','oral tradition','metallurgical analysis'],
 "The Sacred Bell of Great King Seongdeok is the largest surviving bronze bell in Korea: 3.75 m tall and weighing 18.9 tonnes, it was commissioned by King Gyeongdeok of Silla in memory of his father and finally cast in 771 under his son, King Hyegong. Its inscription praises Buddhist teaching. It is famous for its long, deep, pulsing tone and is now displayed at the Gyeongju National Museum. Its popular name, the 'Emille Bell', comes from a legend that repeated castings failed until a poor woman's child was thrown into the molten metal, after which the bell sounded 'emille' - like a child crying for its mother.",
 [('Tested by science','A 1998 analysis of the bell\'s metal composition by a Korean industrial research institute found no detectable phosphorus, the main element of bone, which most take as disproving the sacrifice legend; one of the oldest written versions of the story appears only in Homer Hulbert\'s 1906 book The Passing of Korea.'),
  ('Human sacrifice in Silla?','Archaeologists have found remains of children in Silla-period wells and foundation deposits, and some argue that construction sacrifice was not unknown; this keeps the question of where such legends come from open, even if this bell is not an example.'),
  ('Cross-cultural pattern','Similar "sacrifice to make the work succeed" legends are told of bells in China and of bridges, walls and castles in the Balkans and elsewhere (the Ballad of Master Manole, the Bridge of Arta).')],
 ['wiki:Bell_of_King_Seongdeok','wiki:Gyeongju_National_Museum'],img='Bell of King Seongdeok',dateNote='Cast 771'),
X('mimana-gaya-debate','Gaya and "Mimana": the iron confederacy at the heart of a Korea-Japan history dispute',H,'disputed',2,42,['archaeological','textual'],
 "The Gaya (Kaya) confederacy was a group of small polities in the Nakdong River basin of southern Korea, traditionally dated 42-562 CE, until it was absorbed by Silla. It was rich in iron, which it exported as ingots to the Japanese islands and to the Korean kingdoms, and its royal tombs at Daeseong-dong (Gimhae) and Jisan-dong (Goryeong) have yielded armour, crowns and horse gear. The 8th-century Japanese chronicle Nihon Shoki refers to a region called 'Mimana' and describes a 'Mimana Nihon-fu' that has been read as a Japanese government office in southern Korea.",
 [('Japanese colony theory (rejected)','In the late 19th and early 20th centuries Japanese historians used these passages to claim that the Yamato state ruled southern Korea for two centuries, an idea used to justify the annexation of Korea in 1910. Today historians in both Korea and Japan reject the colonial-administration reading, and in 2010 a joint study group of Japanese and South Korean historians, sponsored by both governments, agreed that Gaya had never been militarily colonised by ancient Japan.'),
  ('Trading post or diplomatic mission','Many scholars now think "Mimana" refers to Gaya or one of its polities, and that the Nihon-fu, if it existed, was a diplomatic or trade presence or a group of Japanese-affiliated people in Gaya. Keyhole-shaped tombs of Japanese type found in south-west Korea add to the debate about who lived where.'),
  ('Influence in reverse','Others emphasise that Gaya iron, potters and horse-riding culture strongly influenced Kofun-period Japan, so that the flow of people and technology was largely from Korea to Japan.')],
 ['wiki:Gaya_confederacy','wiki:Mimana','wiki:Nihon_Shoki'],img='Gaya confederacy',end=562,dateNote='Traditionally 42-562 CE',statusNote='Gaya confirmed; Mimana interpretation disputed'),
X('tripitaka-koreana','The Tripitaka Koreana: 81,000 woodblocks carved to turn back the Mongols',H,'confirmed',2,1237,['archaeological','textual'],
 "The Tripitaka Koreana is the complete Buddhist canon in Classical Chinese carved onto more than 81,000 wooden printing blocks in 13th-century Goryeo Korea and now kept at Haeinsa temple. An earlier set, carved in the 11th century partly as a plea for divine protection against Khitan invasions, was burned during the Mongol invasion of 1232. With the court in refuge on Ganghwa Island, King Gojong ordered a new set as a prayer for the Buddha's help against the Mongols; carving began in 1237 and took about 12 years. It is the oldest intact version of the Chinese Buddhist canon, with about 52 million characters and remarkably few errors, and its 15th-century storage halls at Haeinsa are a UNESCO World Heritage site.",
 [('Did it work?','To the Goryeo court the carving was an act of merit that could turn back invaders; the Mongols did not conquer Goryeo outright but made it a vassal in 1259-1270. Historians see the project as both religious devotion and a statement of national resilience.'),
  ('Natural preservation','The Janggyeong Panjeon halls (1488) use ventilation windows of different sizes, charcoal, lime and clay floors to control humidity naturally; a modern climate-controlled building tested in the 1970s reportedly caused the blocks to deteriorate and was abandoned, a case often cited of old engineering outperforming new.'),
  ('Naming dispute','Some Korean scholars prefer the name Palman Daejanggyeong ("Eighty-thousand Tripitaka") over the English "Tripitaka Koreana".')],
 ['wiki:Tripitaka_Koreana','wiki:Haeinsa'],img='Tripitaka Koreana',end=1248,dateNote='Carved 1237-1248'),
]
# --- part 3: the Arctic, the Caribbean and the Pacific
E+=[
X('thule-migration','The Thule migration: how the ancestors of the Inuit crossed the Arctic in a few generations',H,'confirmed',2,1000,['archaeological','genetic','radiocarbon'],
 "The Thule people, ancestors of all modern Inuit, emerged in coastal Alaska around 1000 CE. Equipped with large skin boats (umiak), kayaks, dog sleds, toggling harpoons and sea-ice hunting techniques, and pursuing bowhead whales whose range expanded in a warmer period, they spread eastward across Arctic Canada with remarkable speed, reaching Greenland by the 13th century. Within a few centuries they had replaced the earlier Dorset (Paleo-Inuit) culture of the eastern Arctic. Their name comes from the Thule district of north-west Greenland, where their remains were first excavated. They met Norse Greenlanders, and Norse objects such as metal pieces and chess-like figures appear in Thule sites.",
 [('What happened to the Dorset?','A 2014 genetic study in Science found that Thule and Dorset populations stayed genetically separate, with no sign of interbreeding; whether the Dorset were displaced by competition, conflict, disease or climate is unknown. Inuit oral tradition remembers an earlier people, the Tuniit, as strong but shy giants who left the land.'),
  ('Drawn by iron?','Some archaeologists argue the rapid eastward push was partly driven by the search for metal, including iron from the Cape York meteorite in Greenland and Norse trade iron; others emphasise whales and climate.'),
  ('Contact with the Norse','The Norse sagas call the people they met Skrælings; the Norse disappeared from Greenland in the 15th century while the Thule-Inuit thrived, a contrast debated in studies of adaptation and collapse.')],
 ['wiki:Thule_people','wiki:Dorset_culture','doi:10.1126/science.1255832'],img='File:Edward Augustus Inglefield photograph of Inuit man with kayak 1854.jpg',end=1300,dateNote='From Alaska c. 1000 CE; Greenland by c. 1200-1300'),
X('qilakitsoq-mummies','The Qilakitsoq mummies: an Inuit family preserved by the Greenland cold (c. 1475)',H,'confirmed',2,1475,['archaeological','radiocarbon','forensic'],
 "In October 1972 two brothers out hunting ptarmigan near Uummannaq in north-west Greenland found graves beneath a rock overhang at Qilakitsoq containing eight naturally freeze-dried bodies: six women of different ages and two children, a boy of about four and a baby of about six months, dressed in fur and skin clothing with spare garments for the journey to the land of the dead. Radiocarbon dating places their deaths around 1475 CE. Studied in Copenhagen from 1978 and returned to Greenland, several are displayed in the Greenland National Museum in Nuuk. They offer an unusually intimate view of Thule-Inuit life five centuries ago.",
 [('Facial tattoos','Five of the six women had facial tattoos, visible with infrared photography: lines on the forehead, cheeks and chin, made by puncturing and by stitching soot-coated sinew under the skin. They are the first certain evidence of this practice in Greenland and match later Inuit traditions of tattoos marking womanhood or marriage.'),
  ('Why so many together?','How the women and children died is unknown; no wounds were found. Suggestions include a boat accident, illness or starvation. The baby may have been buried alive with its dead mother, in line with a later recorded custom when no one could nurse an orphaned infant - an interpretation that remains uncertain.'),
  ('Health clues','Researchers reported that the four-year-old boy had features consistent with Down syndrome and one woman had a nasopharyngeal tumour, and that many had soot in their lungs from lamp smoke.')],
 ['wiki:Qilakitsoq'],img='File:Mummified Qilakitsoq Women (25415797614).jpg',dateNote='Died c. 1475 ± 50; found 1972'),
X('labynkyr-devil','The Labynkyr Devil: a monster in Siberia\'s coldest lake',M,'myth-legend',3,1953,['eyewitness','folklore','speculative'],
 "Lake Labynkyr lies in the Oymyakon district of Yakutia (Sakha Republic), the coldest inhabited region of the Northern Hemisphere, where winter air temperatures fall below -60 °C. Local Evenk and Yakut lore and later visitors speak of a huge dark creature, the 'Labynkyr Devil' (Labynkyrsky chert), said to have attacked dogs and boats. The earliest written account usually cited is from geologist Viktor Tverdokhlebov, who in 1953 wrote of seeing a large dark animal in the nearby Lake Vorota. The lake is unusual in that its water stays at about 2 °C and its surface takes longer to freeze than nearby lakes.",
 [('Relic reptile or giant pike','Proponents have suggested a surviving reptile or amphibian, or an outsized pike; some expedition members reported sonar contacts of large objects in the water.'),
  ('Sceptical view','A 2013 Russian Geographical Society diving expedition under the ice found no monster, only ordinary fish; a diver noted that mirages over ice are common in the north. Fish biologists question whether a lake so cold and poor in food could feed any very large predator.'),
  ('Lake-monster pattern','It joins a global family of lake monsters (Loch Ness, Ogopogo, Lake Van, Champ) in which remote or deep lakes, ambiguous sightings and local lore combine; such reports are not supported by physical evidence.')],
 ['wiki:Labynkyr'],img='Labynkyr',dateNote='First written account 1953; folklore older'),
X('la-navidad-1492','La Navidad: Columbus\'s first fort and the vanished 39 (1492-93)',H,'confirmed',2,1492,['textual','archaeological'],
 "On Christmas Eve 1492 Columbus's flagship Santa María ran aground off the north coast of Hispaniola (today Haiti). With help from the local Taíno cacique Guacanagarí, the crew salvaged the timbers and built a small fort, La Navidad, the first European settlement in the Americas since the Norse. Columbus left about 39 men there and sailed home. When he returned on 27 November 1493, the fort had been burned and all the men were dead. Guacanagarí said they had quarrelled, abused Taíno women and been killed by the rival cacique Caonabo. It foreshadowed the violence of the colonisation that followed.",
 [('Where was it?','The fort\'s exact location has never been confirmed. Archaeologist Kathleen Deagan\'s long excavations at En Bas Saline near Limonade identified a large Taíno town, very likely Guacanagarí\'s village, with a few European objects, but no definitive fort remains.'),
  ('The Santa María "found" in 2014','In 2014 explorer Barry Clifford announced he had found the wreck of the Santa María off Haiti; a UNESCO expert team concluded that the wreck was from a later period and could not be Columbus\'s ship. The real wreck remains undiscovered.'),
  ('Whose fault?','Spanish chroniclers stressed Taíno treachery or the settlers\' misconduct depending on their aims; modern historians see the episode as the first of many clashes born of Spanish demands for gold and women.')],
 ['wiki:La_Navidad','wiki:Santa_María_(ship)'],img='File:Columbus landing on Hispaniola adj.jpg',end=1493,dateNote='Fort built December 1492; found destroyed 27 November 1493'),
X('tula-curacao-1795','Tula\'s revolt: the 1795 slave uprising on Curaçao',H,'confirmed',3,1795,['textual'],
 "On 17 August 1795 an enslaved man named Tula led 40-50 people in an uprising at the Knip plantation in western Curaçao, a Dutch colony. Inspired by the Haitian Revolution and by the news that the Netherlands itself had been taken over by revolutionary France, which had abolished slavery, he argued that the enslaved of Curaçao should also be free. Joined by others under Bastiaan Karpata, the rebels moved from plantation to plantation, freeing hundreds, and defeated a first Dutch force. After about a month they were cornered; Tula was betrayed, captured and publicly tortured to death on 3 October 1795, and Karpata and other leaders were also executed.",
 [('Remembered today','17 August is commemorated on Curaçao as the start of the struggle for freedom, and Tula is honoured with a monument near the place of his execution, the Tula Museum and the 2013 film Tula: The Revolt; other leaders such as Karpata, Louis Mercier and Pedro Wakao are increasingly remembered too.'),
  ('Revolutionary ideas','Historians debate how far Tula and his followers drew on French and Haitian revolutionary ideals versus long-standing local resistance; Tula\'s recorded demands included an end to collective punishment and free Sundays as well as freedom.'),
  ('Colonial records','Almost everything known about the revolt comes from Dutch colonial reports and the accounts of the Franciscan priest Jacobus Schink, who negotiated with Tula; the rebels\' own voices survive only through these filters and through oral memory.')],
 ['wiki:Curaçao_Slave_Revolt_of_1795'],img='Curaçao Slave Revolt of 1795',end=1795,dateNote='17 August - 3 October 1795'),
X('nanny-of-the-maroons','Queen Nanny of the Maroons: the leader who caught bullets',H,'confirmed',2,1728,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "Nanny (c. 1686-c. 1760) led the Windward Maroons, communities of Africans who had escaped slavery and lived in the Blue Mountains of eastern Jamaica. During the First Maroon War (c. 1728-1740) her settlement, Nanny Town, perched above the Stony River gorge, withstood repeated British attacks through guerrilla tactics. After the British made peace with the Leeward Maroons under Cudjoe in 1739, the Windward Maroons signed a treaty in 1740, and Nanny and her people received a grant of 500 acres in Portland parish, where Moore Town (New Nanny Town) still stands. In 1975 Jamaica declared her its only female National Hero; she appears on the 500-dollar note.",
 [('Oral history and legend','Much of what is known comes from Maroon oral tradition: that she was an Asante (Akan) woman, a spiritual leader (obeah) who could catch British bullets and fire them back, and that a boiling cauldron with no fire guarded Nanny Town. Historians treat these as cultural memory of her power and leadership.'),
  ('Documentary traces','Few written sources mention her directly; the 1740 land patent naming "Nanny" is key evidence that a female leader existed. A British soldier\'s claim to have killed an "old obeah woman" earlier is not considered proof of Nanny\'s death.'),
  ('Complicated legacy','The 1739-40 treaties required the Maroons to return future runaways and help suppress slave revolts, which they did in Tacky\'s War (1760); some see this as betrayal, others as survival.')],
 ['wiki:Nanny_of_the_Maroons','wiki:First_Maroon_War'],img='Nanny of the Maroons',end=1740,dateNote='First Maroon War c. 1728-1740; Nanny c. 1686-c. 1760'),
X('white-witch-rose-hall','The White Witch of Rose Hall: Jamaica\'s haunted great house and a novel taken for history',M,'debunked',3,1831,['textual','folklore'],
 "Rose Hall, a Georgian plantation great house near Montego Bay, Jamaica, is famous for the legend of Annie Palmer, the 'White Witch': a mistress raised in Haiti and taught voodoo, who supposedly murdered three husbands and many enslaved lovers before being strangled by an enslaved man around 1831. Her ghost is said to haunt the house, now a popular tourist attraction with night-time ghost tours, and the story inspired Johnny Cash's 'The Ballad of Annie Palmer' and many television ghost-hunting episodes.",
 [('A 1929 novel','Investigations, including one by Benjamin Radford in 2007, concluded that the tale is largely modelled on Herbert G. de Lisser\'s popular 1929 novel The White Witch of Rosehall. The real Annie Palmer (née Paterson) who lived at Rose Hall was not murdered and neither of her husbands is known to have been killed.'),
  ('Confused identities','The legend may have merged the real Annie Palmer with Rosa Palmer, an earlier mistress of Rose Hall who had four husbands but was remembered as virtuous, and with an earlier, sketchy report of a cruel mistress strangled on a neighbouring estate during the 1831 Baptist War.'),
  ('Believers\' view','Staff, visitors and TV psychics report apparitions, footsteps and cold spots; supporters say the legend preserves memory of plantation violence even if names are wrong.')],
 ['wiki:Annie_Palmer_(White_Witch_of_Rose_Hall)','wiki:H._G._de_Lisser'],img='Annie Palmer (White Witch of Rose Hall)',dateNote='Legend set c. 1820s-1831; novel 1929'),
X('laperouse-vanikoro','Lapérouse: the expedition that vanished in the Pacific (1788)',H,'confirmed',2,1788,['textual','archaeological','underwater archaeology'],
 "In 1785 Louis XVI sent Jean-François de Galaup, comte de Lapérouse, with two ships, Boussole and Astrolabe, on a great scientific voyage to rival Cook's. They visited Chile, Easter Island, Hawaii, Alaska, California, Macau, the Philippines, Korea, Kamchatka and Samoa, sending journals home along the way, and in January 1788 arrived at Botany Bay just as the British First Fleet was founding Sydney. On 10 March 1788 they sailed into the Pacific and were never seen by Europeans again. In 1826 the Irish captain Peter Dillon found French anchors, cannons and objects on Vanikoro in the Santa Cruz Islands (Solomon Islands); both ships had wrecked on its reefs. A wreck found in 1964 was identified as Boussole in 2005.",
 [('Survivors left behind','Islanders told Dillon that survivors had built a small boat and left, while a few men stayed. In 1791 HMS Pandora, hunting the Bounty mutineers, passed Vanikoro and saw smoke signals but sailed on; some historians think these came from French survivors still alive three years after the wreck.'),
  ('"Any news of Lapérouse?"','Louis XVI is said to have asked this on the morning of his execution in January 1793; the anecdote is widely repeated but its reliability is uncertain.'),
  ('Remains identified','In 2003 a complete skeleton was found in the Boussole wreck site, and French researchers have used forensic facial reconstruction to try to identify which member of the expedition he was.')],
 ['wiki:Jean-François_de_Galaup,_comte_de_Lapérouse','wiki:Vanikoro'],img='File:Laperouse - btv1b10500946s.jpg',dateNote='Left Botany Bay 10 March 1788; wreck site found 1826'),
X('blackbeard-1718','Blackbeard: the pirate, the burning fuses and the wreck of Queen Anne\'s Revenge',H,'confirmed',2,1716,['textual','underwater archaeology'],
 "Edward Teach or Thatch (c. 1680-1718), known as Blackbeard, operated in the Caribbean and off the American coast at the height of the 'Golden Age of Piracy'. In 1717 he captured the French slave ship La Concorde, renamed her Queen Anne's Revenge and armed her with 40 guns. In 1718 he blockaded Charleston, South Carolina, holding prominent citizens hostage for a chest of medicines. Soon after, the Queen Anne's Revenge ran aground near Beaufort, North Carolina. On 22 November 1718 (Old Style) a force sent by Virginia's governor under Lieutenant Robert Maynard caught him at Ocracoke Inlet; Teach was killed in hand-to-hand fighting and his head hung from Maynard's bowsprit.",
 [('The image','The famous picture of Blackbeard with lit slow-match fuses under his hat and pistols across his chest comes from Captain Charles Johnson\'s A General History of the Pyrates (1724), whose author is unidentified and whose accounts mix fact and embellishment. Historians debate how much of his fearsome reputation was deliberate theatre to make victims surrender without a fight; there is no evidence he killed anyone before his final battle.'),
  ('Buried treasure','Legends of his buried gold have sent treasure hunters along the US east coast for centuries, but nothing found has been linked to him; historians note there is almost no evidence pirates buried treasure, an idea popularised by Treasure Island.'),
  ('The wreck','A wreck found in 1996 in Beaufort Inlet has been identified by North Carolina archaeologists as Queen Anne\'s Revenge, based on its guns, date and location, though some questioned the identification at first; hundreds of thousands of artefacts have been raised.')],
 ['wiki:Blackbeard','wiki:Queen_Anne%27s_Revenge'.replace('%27',"'")],img='Blackbeard',end=1718,dateNote='Active c. 1716-1718; killed 22 November 1718 (O.S.)'),
X('tui-tonga-maritime-empire','The Tuʻi Tonga: a Polynesian maritime "empire"?',H,'disputed',2,950,['oral tradition','archaeological','linguistic'],
 "Tongan tradition traces the sacred kings, the Tuʻi Tonga, to ʻAhoʻeitu, son of the sky god Tangaloa and a mortal woman, around 950 CE. From their capital at Muʻa/Lapaha on Tongatapu, where large stone-faced royal tombs (langi) survive, later Tuʻi Tonga are said to have extended their influence by voyaging canoe over a wide area of western Polynesia, receiving tribute and marriage alliances. Archaeological and linguistic studies show strong Tongan influence in ʻUvea (Wallis), Futuna, Niuatoputapu, Niue, Rotuma and parts of Samoa and Fiji between about 1200 and 1500. Samoan tradition recounts that the Tongans were driven out of Samoa by brothers ancestral to the Malietoa line. Over time the Tuʻi Tonga became a sacred figure while temporal power passed to other lines, the Tuʻi Haʻatakalaua and the Tuʻi Kanokupolu, from whom today's Tongan royal family descends.",
 [('Empire or maritime chiefdom','Some scholars describe a true "maritime empire" with tribute and governors; others prefer "maritime chiefdom" or network of kin and exchange, arguing that the evidence shows influence and prestige rather than administrative control.'),
  ('Oral history','Much of the narrative rests on genealogies and oral traditions recorded after European contact, which can preserve real events but also reflect later political claims.'),
  ('Pacific pattern','Similar sacred-king and voyaging networks are known from Pohnpei (Nan Madol) and Hawaii; Tonga is the only Pacific monarchy never formally colonised, which shapes how this history is told today.')],
 ['wiki:Tuʻi_Tonga_Empire','wiki:Tuʻi_Tonga'],img='File:Paepae ʻo Teleʻa.jpg',end=1500,dateNote='Traditionally from c. 950; peak influence c. 1200-1500',statusNote='Tongan influence confirmed; scale as "empire" debated'),
]
